<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_drop_graph.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-dropgraph">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-dropgraph">
  <primary>DROP GRAPH</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>DROP GRAPH</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>DROP GRAPH</refname>
  <refpurpose>drop a graph and, with CASCADE, everything in it</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
DROP GRAPH [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">graph_name</replaceable> [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropgraph-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP GRAPH</command> drops the graph's catalog row and its schema. Everything the
   graph holds depends on that schema: labels, the tables behind them, their rows, indexes,
   constraints and sequences. So <literal>RESTRICT</literal>, which is the default, refuses
   while any of it is there.
  </para>

  <para>
   A graph made by the plain <xref linkend="cypher-creategraph"/> is never empty.
   <literal>ag_vertex</literal>, <literal>ag_edge</literal> and
   <literal>ag_label_seq</literal> are created with it, so every such graph needs
   <literal>CASCADE</literal>. Only a graph made with <literal>CREATE GRAPH ONLY</literal>,
   which creates none of those, can be dropped without it.
  </para>

  <para>
   Dropping a graph does not require its labels to be empty.
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> takes the rows with the tables.
  </para>

  <para>
   If the dropped graph is the session's <varname>graph_path</varname>, the setting is
   cleared.
  </para>

  <para>
   The graph's schema cannot be dropped on its own. <command>DROP SCHEMA</command> on it is
   refused with <literal>cannot drop schema clearance because graph clearance requires
   it</literal>, so the catalog row and the schema always go together.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropgraph-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error for a graph that is not there. Other names in the
      same statement are still dropped.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">graph_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the graph to drop. Several names may be given, separated by commas.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CASCADE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Drop the objects that depend on the graph: its labels, their tables and rows, and the
      label id sequence.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>RESTRICT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refuse if anything depends on the graph. This is the default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropgraph-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build graphs of their own and drop them at the end. The first one holds a label and a
   vertex:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH clearance;
SET graph_path = clearance;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE (:film {title: 'Arrival'});
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Without <literal>CASCADE</literal> the drop is refused, and the message lists what stands
   in the way:
<programlisting>
DROP GRAPH clearance;

ERROR:  cannot drop graph clearance because other objects depend on it
DETAIL:  sequence clearance.ag_label_seq depends on schema clearance
vlabel ag_vertex depends on schema clearance
elabel ag_edge depends on schema clearance
vlabel film depends on schema clearance
HINT:  Use DROP ... CASCADE to drop the dependent objects too.
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> takes all four, and the vertex in <literal>film</literal> with
   them:
<programlisting>
DROP GRAPH clearance CASCADE;

NOTICE:  drop cascades to 4 other objects
DETAIL:  drop cascades to sequence clearance.ag_label_seq
drop cascades to vlabel ag_vertex
drop cascades to elabel ag_edge
drop cascades to vlabel film
DROP GRAPH
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>clearance</literal> was the session's <varname>graph_path</varname>, so the
   setting is now empty:
<programlisting>
SHOW graph_path;

 graph_path
------------

(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Several graphs go in one statement, and <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> reports the name that
   is not there instead of failing on it:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH archive_2024;
CREATE GRAPH archive_2025;
DROP GRAPH IF EXISTS archive_2024, archive_2025, archive_2026 CASCADE;

NOTICE:  graph "archive_2026" does not exist, skipping
NOTICE:  drop cascades to 6 other objects
DETAIL:  drop cascades to sequence archive_2025.ag_label_seq
drop cascades to vlabel ag_vertex
drop cascades to elabel ag_edge
drop cascades to sequence archive_2024.ag_label_seq
drop cascades to vlabel ag_vertex
drop cascades to elabel ag_edge
DROP GRAPH
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropgraph-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   To empty a graph without dropping it, run <literal>MATCH (n) DETACH DELETE n</literal> and
   then drop the labels that are no longer wanted. The schema and the base labels stay in
   place. A label has to be empty before <xref linkend="cypher-dropvlabel"/> will remove it,
   so that delete is the usual first step. <literal>DROP GRAPH ... CASCADE</literal> skips
   the requirement altogether, because it drops the tables rather than the labels one at a
   time.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropgraph-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 defines the drop graph statement in clause 12.5 as
   <literal>DROP [PROPERTY] GRAPH [IF EXISTS]</literal> with one name. AgensGraph accepts the
   <literal>DROP GRAPH</literal> and <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> parts of that. It adds a
   name list and the <literal>CASCADE</literal> or <literal>RESTRICT</literal> choice, which
   the standard does not have. The <literal>PROPERTY</literal> spelling is not accepted.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DROP GRAPH</command> corresponds to <command>DROP SCHEMA</command>, and
   <literal>IF EXISTS</literal>, the name list and
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> or <literal>RESTRICT</literal> behave as they do there. It
   differs in refusing to leave a half dropped graph behind, since the catalog row and the
   schema can only be dropped together.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

